[
    {
        "day":  1,
        "concept_title":  "Probability Basics",
        "theme":  "Statistics",
        "kindergarten_explanation":  "Flip a coin. It can land heads or tails, but you cannot know before you flip. Probability is organized counting of what could happen. If a jar has 5 red and 5 blue marbles, you have a 1 in 2 chance for each color. If there are 4 equally likely outcomes, each is 1 in 4. The more you know about the situation, the better your guess.",
        "why_it_matters":  "Probability helps you make smarter choices, from packing an umbrella when rain is likely to picking the faster checkout line.",
        "connections_unlocked":  "Leads to Day 2: Bayesian Statistics. Leads to Day 15: Game Theory Basics. Related to Day 39: Monte Carlo Methods.",
        "quick_exercise":  "What is the probability you will eat pizza this week? List three reasons for and three reasons against, then decide if it feels more likely or less likely than 50 percent."
    },
    {
        "day":  2,
        "concept_title":  "Bayesian Statistics",
        "theme":  "Statistics",
        "kindergarten_explanation":  "Start with a guess, then update it when you get new clues. If you think there is a 30 percent chance of rain and you see dark clouds, you should raise your guess. You keep your old belief, but blend it with the new evidence. That is Bayesian thinking: you get smarter every time you learn something.",
        "why_it_matters":  "Bayesian thinking powers everything from spam filters to medical tests because it updates beliefs instead of starting from scratch.",
        "connections_unlocked":  "Builds on Day 1: Probability Basics. Leads to Day 6: Optimal Foraging Theory. Related to Day 40: Markov Chains.",
        "quick_exercise":  "You think there is a 20 percent chance your friend will call tonight. Then you remember it is their birthday. How should your guess change?"
    },
    {
        "day":  3,
        "concept_title":  "Power Laws",
        "theme":  "Systems",
        "kindergarten_explanation":  "In many systems, a few things are huge while most are small. A few videos get most of the views, a few cities hold most people, and a few earthquakes release most energy. This is a power law. It often happens when success feeds more success, like a snowball that grows faster once it gets big.",
        "why_it_matters":  "Power laws explain why a small number of causes create most of the impact, from wealth to attention.",
        "connections_unlocked":  "Leads to Day 4: Emergent Complexity. Leads to Day 21: Pareto Principle. Related to Day 20: Network Effects.",
        "quick_exercise":  "List five things you use. Which one gets most of your attention? Does a small number of items dominate your time?"
    },
    {
        "day":  4,
        "concept_title":  "Emergent Complexity",
        "theme":  "Systems",
        "kindergarten_explanation":  "Give many parts a few simple rules and surprising patterns can appear. Ants follow simple trail rules, yet a colony builds complex nests. Drivers follow basic rules, yet traffic jams form. Emergent complexity means the whole can do things no single part can predict.",
        "why_it_matters":  "In complex systems, changing the rules of interaction often matters more than controlling individual parts.",
        "connections_unlocked":  "Builds on Day 3: Power Laws. Leads to Day 5: State Changes. Related to Day 17: Chaos Theory.",
        "quick_exercise":  "Watch a flock of birds or a school of fish. What simple rule might each individual follow to create the group motion?"
    },
    {
        "day":  5,
        "concept_title":  "State Changes (Phase Transitions)",
        "theme":  "Systems",
        "kindergarten_explanation":  "Slow changes can push a system to a tipping point, then it suddenly flips. Heat ice slowly and it stays ice, then it melts. Add cars to a road and traffic flows, then it jams. A state change is a dramatic shift after small steps add up.",
        "why_it_matters":  "Spotting tipping points helps you prevent bad shifts or trigger good ones before it is too late.",
        "connections_unlocked":  "Builds on Day 4: Emergent Complexity. Leads to Day 23: Tipping Points. Related to Day 34: Second Law of Thermodynamics.",
        "quick_exercise":  "Think of a moment that changed suddenly after building slowly, like learning a skill or losing patience. What was the tipping point?"
    },
